      'Under the EU/EEA/CH framework agreement on habitual cross-border telework (Art. 16 Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 derogation): under 25% of working time teleworked in the residence state, the framework does not apply: the employer’s state normally stays competent, and regular work in two states must be reported to the residence-state institution, which determines the legislation. From 25% to under 50%, the residence state normally becomes competent unless employer and employee request an A1 keeping the employer’s state. Regulation (EC) No 987/2009 Art. 14(8) and (10) set the “substantial part” indicator (under 25%) and the 12-calendar-month projection for that assessment. The A1 lasts up to 3 years and can reach back at most 3 months, if contributions were already paid there. At 50% or more the framework is not available. It does not cover people who habitually work other than by telework in the residence state, habitually work in a state other than the residence state and the employer’s state, are self-employed, or have employers in more than one state. Telework here means work done through an IT link. Sources: Belgian FPS Social Security (depositary); Framework Agreement text and explanatory memorandum; Regulation (EC) No 883/2004 Art. 13; Regulation (EC) No 987/2009 Art. 14(8)/(10); CLEISS.',
