    basel_de_nonreturn_60: 'Non-return days for work (up to 60 a year)',
    basel_de_nonreturn_note:
      'Germany–Switzerland: cross-border status is lost above 60 work-related non-return days a year (DBA DE–CH Art. 15a; Konsultationsvereinbarung). Counted here: days you mark “stayed away overnight for work”, plus every day of work outside Germany and Switzerland, even with same-day return. Same-day trips inside Germany or Switzerland, illness and accident do not count. On multi-day business trips paid by the employer, weekends and holidays count too, so log them as trip days. Part-time staff working only certain days have a proportionally lower limit (KonsVerCHEV §9(2): contract days against 250 for a 5-day week, 300 for a 6-day week; holidays are not deducted). Enter your contract days per year and the counter shows the proportional figure against 250 as an estimate, rounded down, because the ordinance gives no rounding rule. Shorter hours on every working day are not covered by this cut. Part-year work also lowers the limit (§10(2): “nach entsprechender Kürzung”), but the ordinance gives no formula, so BorderDay does not apply one: if you work only part of the year, the 60 shown can be too high, so check your limit. Several employers are assessed together. Full days worked at your home in Germany are not Nichtrückkehrtage under the ESTV/BMF Konsultationsvereinbarung of 26 July 2022. The employer files form Gre-3 (31 March in Aargau; check your canton). Sources: Fedlex Art. 15a; ESTV Konsultationsvereinbarung DE–CH (PDF); KonsVerCHEV §§ 8–9; Canton Aargau leaflet, status 1 Jan 2026.',
