    be_fr_pre2012_30: 'Days outside the Belgian border zone (max 30/year, pre-2012 frontier workers)',
    be_fr_pre2012_note:
      'Only for people who were frontier workers under the France–Belgium regime before 1 January 2012. Until 2033 they keep a 30-day annual limit for work outside the Belgian border zone. Work counts as inside the zone only when you are physically there, so telework from outside the zone, including from home in France, uses the 30-day tolerance. Any exit from the zone, even a short one, counts as a whole day, so a half-day counts as 1 here. Not counted (BOFiP §130, list not exhaustive): force majeure beyond your and your employer’s control, occasional transit through the rest of Belgium, trade-union delegate activity, works-council, joint-committee, prevention-committee and employers’-federation meetings, staff parties, medical visits, vocational training of up to 5 working days a calendar year, and certain transport work; BorderDay cannot separate these, so do not log those days as telework or trips. BorderDay counts telework and trip days as a proxy and cannot verify Belgian border-zone geography: on-site days outside the zone but inside Belgium are not captured. First breach of the 30 days: the regime is lost for that year. Second breach: it is lost permanently. Sources: French minister’s answer of 28 May 2024; BOFiP.',
